h a runtime_checkable protocol, it checks for method presence (not signatures). FakeFlightController has all methods, so it passes. Good.\n\nBut wait — the actual implementation does NOT import FlightController, so it's just a standalone class. That's fine - the plan's version imported it but didn't actually use it for typing the class. Actually the plan shows it imported but the class declaration doesn't say `(FlightController)`. So the import was unused in the plan's version too! In the actual version, they removed the unused import. That's actually a cleaner change (DRY/YAGNI - no unused import). Good, that's an improvement, not a finding.\n\nNow, there's another subtle issue. The plan uses `from typing import TYPE_CHECKING` placed in the middle of the file, whereas the actual implementation puts `TYPE_CHECKING` import at the top with `from typing import Protocol, runtime_checkable, TYPE_CHECKING`. That's a fine refactor.\n\nNow the key concern: Does FakeFlightController actually satisfy the FlightController protocol? runtime_checkable protocols only check for attribute/method existence, not signatures. FakeFlightController has: arm, takeoff, land, goto, step, get_pose, get_battery, is_armed. All match. Good.\n\nLet me verify the plan's FakeFlightController implementation matches the implemented version (the kinematics). Let me read the rest.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8aad9222cc9362ce","name":"read","arguments":{},"partialArgs":"{","streamIndex":0}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"tencent/hy3","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1785145813573,"responseId":"gen-1785145813-o96RsI5tzqLXXJOvfrvD"}}
