 `_landing` a finding? I don't think it rises to blocking. I'll leave it out of findings.\n\nLet me also double check: the implementation imports `TYPE_CHECKING` at top, matching plan's intent (plan put it mid-file; actual moved to top — both fine).\n\nOne more: `MissionPlanner` references `MissionState` which doesn't exist yet (it's a future task, Task for mission/state.py). Under TYPE_CHECKING, this is fine because type checkers resolve it from a not-yet-created module. But wait — when running the actual gate's typecheck (pyright), will it error that `drone.mission.state` doesn't exist? The plan noted `# pyright: ignore[reportMissingImports]`. The actual implementation has `from drone.mission.state import MissionState  # pyright: ignore[reportMissingImports]`. Good, they kept the ignore comment. So typecheck won't fail.\n\nBut actually — is `drone.mission` package even importable? For pyright, missing module with ignore comment is fine. Good.\n\nNow, the deterministic gate (lint + types + unit) — I can't run it (bash disabled). But the review is about my evaluation. The skill says don't claim dod_met without checking. I've read the tests and implementation. The tests are straightforward and would pass. But I genuinely cannot run them. I should base dod_met on the evidence I have.\n\nLet me consider: are there any tests that would fail? \n- `test_satisfies_flight_controller_protocol`: isinstance check","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"tencent/hy3","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1785145819874,"responseId":"gen-1785145820-byd5LU1798dc5VOUpByg"}}
